  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Around Le Lavandou and Bormes-les-Mimosas, dog-friendly options are scarce. Right past the fishermen's huts leading to the Fort de Brégançon, the small pebble beach of Brégançon looks out over Cabasson bay and the Giens peninsula. The Bormes-les-Mimosas tourist office states there that "animals are welcome", and specifies on its beaches page that "our four-legged friends are allowed on the Brégançon and Gouron beaches all year round".</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Access is on foot only from Cabasson beach, with compulsory paid parking at Parc Capalo (April to October). Don't confuse this with <a href="/en/fiche-plage-cabasson-bormes-les-mimosas">Cabasson beach</a> itself, right next door: its tourist office page states that animals are not accepted there. The office gives no leash rule for Brégançon beach: as a precaution, keep your dog on a leash. Also, animals are not allowed on the guided tour of the Fort de Brégançon.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the coastal path starting from Cabasson is a lovely walk to add with your dog; check the trail's rules before setting out.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-plage-gouron-bormes-les-mimosas">Gouron beach</a>, where the tourist office says dogs are welcome year-round (the office FAQ specifies: part of the beach), lies at the entrance to the port of Bormes-les-Mimosas.</p>
    </div>

